{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.drift_is_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.drift_is_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":202,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L202-L207","doc":"The drift itself is second order: it vanishes to first order in `u`.\nStated as the exact quadratic dependence, which is what makes the derived\ndrift an order of magnitude smaller than a first-order one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.drift_ratio_exactly_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.drift_ratio_exactly_four","kind":"theorem","line":185,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L185-L200","doc":"**The `u` vs `2u` branches differ by exactly a factor of 4.**\n\nWhether the substitution is `u = ln q₀` or `u = 2 ln q₀` is the NZ-1/2 binary\nrecorded in bean `qou-yh67`, which states it cannot be settled numerically.\nThis theorem says the RATIO of the two resulting drifts is forced to be 4\nregardless — because the normalisation enters `τ` squared — so the binary\nmoves the drift by a known factor and nothing else.\n\nHolds for all `c₁ c₃ u`, including the degenerate cases, since both sides\nscale together. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.tauQuad","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.tauQuad","kind":"def","line":180,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L180-L183","doc":"The cusp shape to second order, with both coefficients free. Deliberately\nNOT specialised to `c₁ = 2√-3`, `c₃ = c₁/3` — those are numerical results of\nthe probe, not theorems of this file. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.lattice_index_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.lattice_index_four","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L172-L175","doc":"The index of that sublattice is 4: the change-of-basis matrix from\n`(1, ω)` to `(1, τ₀)` is `[[1, 0], [-2, 4]]`, of determinant 4. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.tau0_eq_lattice_point","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.tau0_eq_lattice_point","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L157-L170","doc":"**The exact cross-check: `τ₀ = -2 + 4ω`.**\n\nTwo independent sources describe one lattice. The corpus proves the 4₁ cusp\nlattice is the hexagonal `ℤ⟨1, ω⟩`; NZ give the cusp SHAPE as `2√-3`. This\nidentity says NZ's meridian-longitude lattice `ℤ⟨1, τ₀⟩` is a SUBLATTICE of\nit, generated by `1` and `-2 + 4ω`.\n\nNote it holds for EVERY `s` — no discriminant hypothesis — because it is a\nstatement about the change of basis, not about which quadratic field one is\nin. That is why it is an exact integer relation rather than a numerical\nagreement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.omega_primitive_sixth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.omega_primitive_sixth","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L148-L155","doc":"**`ω` is a primitive 6th root of unity**, i.e. a root of the 6th\ncyclotomic polynomial `X² - X + 1`. Needs the discriminant `s² = 3`; this is\nwhere the hexagonal structure comes from. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.tau0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.tau0","kind":"def","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L144-L146","doc":"Neumann-Zagier's cusp shape for 4₁: `τ₀ = 2√-3`, written with the\ndiscriminant explicit. With `s² = 3` this is `2i√3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.omega","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.omega","kind":"def","line":140,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L140-L142","doc":"The corpus's cusp shape: a primitive 6th root of unity, written with its\ndiscriminant explicit. With `s² = 3` this is `e^{iπ/3} = (1 + i√3)/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.tauShape_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.tauShape_even","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L128-L133","doc":"The even-ness of `τ` in the form actually used downstream: the first-order\ncoefficient of an even function vanishes, so writing `τ(u) = c₁ + c₃u²` omits\nno `u¹` term. Stated as the parity identity for the quadratic shape. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.oddFun_div_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.oddFun_div_even","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L114-L126","doc":"**An odd `v` gives an even `τ(u) = v(u)/u`.**\n\nThe cusp shape is the ratio of the longitude to the meridian log-holonomy.\nWhen the numerator is odd — which §1 establishes for 4₁ — the ratio is even,\nso its expansion about `u = 0` has no linear term and the leading correction\nto `τ₀` is `O(u²)`.\n\nThis is the structural half of the probe's finding that the real modulus\ndrift is second order: the quadratic suppression is forced by parity, not\nobserved in the numbers. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.gluing_symm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.gluing_symm","kind":"theorem","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L104-L110","doc":"The swap is an involution on the gluing variety: if `(z, w)` is a\nsolution then so is `(w, z)`. This is what makes `v ↦ -v` a symmetry of the\ndeformation rather than merely a reparametrisation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.gluing_swap_inverse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.NeumannZagierCuspParity.gluing_swap_inverse","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/NeumannZagierCuspParity.lean#L87-L102","doc":"**The reciprocal-argument identity.**\n\nIf `z, w` lie on the 4₁ gluing variety `z w (z-1)(w-1) = 1`, then the two\nlogarithm arguments produced by `v ↦ -v` (which swaps `z ↔ w`) are reciprocal:\n\n    (z(1-w)) · (w(1-z)) = 1.\n\nHence `u(-v) = log(z(1-w)) = -log(w(1-z)) = -u(v)`: the meridian\nlog-holonomy is an ODD function of the longitude one, which is NZ eq (52)\nfor this knot. Stated over a generic commutative ring because the identity\nis algebraic — it uses only `(1-w)(1-z) = (w-1)(z-1)` and needs no analysis,\nno branch choice, and no series. ","signatureChunk":0}]}